ore a body part, a family member, a pet (especially of someone previously mentioned), as an alternative to a possessive pronoun.","example":"A stone hit him on the head. (= “A stone hit him on his head.”)","frame_run_id":5,"frame_seq":4,"frame_ts_ms":1792285133262,"gram_function":[],"id":"the|determiner|4","lemma":"the","mw":false,"norm":"the","pos":"determiner","rel_prior":0.2,"score":0.5,"semantic_atoms":["ety:1","pos_raw:article"],"source":"active_cells","synonyms":[]},{"activation":0.25,"antonyms":[],"definition":"(colloquial) Used before a noun phrase, including a simple noun — The definite grammatical article that shows that the noun phrase that immediately follows it is definitely identifiable... — ...because it is presumed to be definitely known in context or from shared knowledge. — Precedes a familiar nickname or other term of address.","example":"How's the Sal today?","frame_run_id":5,"frame_seq":4,"frame_ts_ms":1792285133262,"gram_function":[],"id":"the|determiner|5","lemma":"the","mw":false,"norm":"the","pos":"determiner","rel_prior":0.2,"score":0.5,"semantic_atoms":["ety:1","tag:colloquial","pos_raw:article"],"source":"active_cells","synonyms":[]},{"activation":0.25,"antonyms":[],"definition":"Used before a noun phrase, including a simple noun — The definite grammatical article that shows that the noun phrase that immediately follows it is definitely identifiable... — ...because it is presumed to be definitely known in context or from shared knowledge. — Used in many idiomatic expressions and proverbs to refer to common objects, roles, or situations connected with something definite, in the manner of an analogy","example":"square the circle; feel the pinch; beat around the bush; throw the baby out with the bathwater","frame_run_id":5,"frame_seq":4,"frame_ts_ms":1792285133262,"gram_function":[],"id":"the|determiner|6","lemma":"the","mw":false,"norm":"the","pos":"determiner","rel_prior":0.2,"score":0.5,"semantic_atoms":["ety:1","pos_raw:article"],"source":"active_cells","synonyms":[]},{"activation":0.25,"antonyms":[],"definition":"Used before a noun phrase, including a simple noun — When stressed, indicates that it describes something which is considered to be best or exclusively worthy of attention.","example":"That is the hospital to go to for heart surgery.","frame_run_id":5,"frame_seq":4,"frame_ts_ms":1792285133262,"gram_function":[],"id":"the|determiner|7","lemma":"the","mw":false,"norm":"the","pos":"determiner","rel_prior":0.2,"score":0.5,"semantic_atoms":["ety:1","pos_raw:article"],"source":"active_cells","synonyms":[]},{"activation":0.25,"antonyms":[],"definition":"Used before a noun phrase, including a simple noun — Used before a noun phrase beginning with superlative or comparative adjective or an ordinal number, indicating that the noun refers to a single item.","example":"That was the juiciest apple pie ever.","frame_run_id":5,"frame_seq":4,"frame_ts_ms":1792285133262,"gram_function":[],"id":"the|determiner|8","lemma":"the","mw":false,"norm":"the","pos":"determiner","rel_prior":0.2,"score":0.5,"semantic_atoms":["ety:1","pos_raw:article"],"source":"active_cells","synonyms":[]},{"activation":0.25,"antonyms":[],"definition":"Used before a noun phrase, including a simple noun — Introducing a singular term to be taken generically: preceding a name of something standing for a whole class.","example":"Stern and God-fearing, the Afrikaner takes his religion seriously.","frame_run_id":5,"frame_seq":4,"frame_ts_ms":1792285133262,"gram_function":[],"id":"the|determiner|9","lemma":"the","mw":false,"norm":"the","pos":"determiner","rel_prior":0.2,"score":0.5,"semantic_atoms":["ety:1","pos_raw:article"],"source":"active_cells","synonyms":[]},{"activation":0.25,"antonyms":[],"definition":"Used before a noun phrase, including a simple noun — Used with the plural of a surname to indicate the entire family.","example":"The Bushes have held political office for several decades and the Kennedys longer.","frame_run_id":5,"fr