aning and can be expressed by itself; the smallest discrete, meaningful unit of language. (contrast morpheme.) — The smallest discrete unit of written language with a particular meaning, composed of one or more letters or symbols and one or more morphemes","example":"Polonius: What do you read, my lord?\nHamlet: Words, words, words.","frame_run_id":38,"frame_seq":37,"frame_ts_ms":1792292295353,"gram_function":[],"id":"word|noun|3","lemma":"word","mw":false,"norm":"word","pos":"noun","rel_prior":0.2,"score":0.5,"semantic_atoms":["ety:1","tag:countable","tag:uncountable","pos_raw:noun"],"source":"active_cells","synonyms":[]},{"activation":0.25,"antonyms":[],"definition":"(countable, uncountable) The smallest unit of language that has a particular meaning and can be expressed by itself; the smallest discrete, meaningful unit of language. (contrast morpheme.) — A discrete, meaningful unit of language approved by an authority or native speaker (compare non-word).","example":"“Ain’t! How often am I to tell you ain’t ain’t a word?”","frame_run_id":38,"frame_seq":37,"frame_ts_ms":1792292295353,"gram_function":[],"id":"word|noun|4","lemma":"word","mw":false,"norm":"word","pos":"noun","rel_prior":0.2,"score":0.5,"semantic_atoms":["ety:1","tag:countable","tag:uncountable","pos_raw:noun"],"source":"active_cells","synonyms":[]},{"activation":0.25,"antonyms":[],"definition":"(countable, uncountable) Something like such a unit of language: — A sequence of letters, characters, or sounds, considered as a discrete entity, though it does not necessarily belong to a language or have a meaning.","example":"In still another variation, the nonsense word is presented and the teacher asks, \"What sound was in the beginning of the word?\" \"In the middle?\" and so on. The child should always respond with the phoneme; he should not use letter labels.","frame_run_id":38,"frame_seq":37,"frame_ts_ms":1792292295353,"gram_function":[],"id":"word|noun|5","lemma":"word","mw":false,"norm":"word","pos